# These files are read by Linux, not by Windows, regardless of who edits them.
# A shell script checked out with CRLF fails as `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`,
# and the error names the interpreter rather than the line endings — so pin the
# endings here instead of losing an afternoon to it later.
*.sh            text eol=lf
deploy/**       text eol=lf
# `supabase/**.sql` stood here and matched NOTHING: in gitattributes a bare
# `**` does not cross directory boundaries, so it never reached
# supabase/migrations/*.sql — `git check-attr text eol` answered `unspecified`
# for every migration. Measured 22.10.2026: with core.autocrlf on, the blob
# holds LF while `git archive HEAD` exported 2603 CR bytes, so the RLS kit on
# the VPS has been reading CRLF SQL all along. PostgreSQL does not care, but a
# probe that pins a fragment of a stored function body does: one aborted the
# whole kit run, and the kit stops at the first failure, so every file after it
# went unrun and read as passing.
*.sql           text eol=lf

# vitest rewrites a snapshot with the platform's line endings, so on a fresh
# Windows checkout the golden masters appear modified with **no content change** —
# which reads as "the payroll snapshots moved" and is the one false alarm a
# characterization test cannot afford. Pinned to LF so the alarm only ever means
# what it says.
*.snap          text eol=lf
